course: dictionary
lesson: 2377
title: Cupboard: a place to keep things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A cupboard is a piece of furniture, or a space in a wall, with doors and shelves. You keep things inside it, such as cups, plates, food or clothes. «Open the cupboard under the kitchen sink» tells you where to look: a cupboard is something you open to find things.
explain: Context helps you work out what is kept there. «Mum opens the cupboard and finds tiny dark droppings like grains of rice and a chewed hole in the cereal packet» shows that the cereal packet is kept in the cupboard. Try the swap: if you can put "cupboard" in place of a word and it still makes sense, you are on the right track.
explain: Some words look the same but mean something else. «A cabinet is a team, not a cupboard» tells you that cabinet here means a team of ministers, not a piece of furniture. The sentence gives you the clue: it says it is not a cupboard.
explain: Cupboards also turn up in stories. «The console went in the cupboard for a week» means Ollie put it away out of sight. The word tells you where it was hidden, and the rest of the sentence tells you why.
fact: A cupboard is a piece of furniture or a space in a wall with doors and shelves.
fact: People keep cups, plates, food and clothes in cupboards.
fact: Cleaning things, such as bleach, should be kept in a high or locked cupboard, away from little children.
fact: «Cupboard» can be plural: one cupboard, many cupboards.
fact: «Cabinet» can mean a cupboard, but in «a cabinet is a team» it means a group of ministers who run things like health, schools and money.
exercise: What is a cupboard? | A kind of bird | A team of ministers who run things | A long, narrow road | *A piece of furniture or a space in a wall with doors and shelves
exercise: Dad says bleach must be kept somewhere safe. Where should it go? | On the table | *In a high or locked cupboard | In the bath | Under your pillow
exercise: In «a cabinet is a team, not a cupboard», what does cabinet mean? | *A team of ministers who run things | A piece of furniture with doors | A kind of food | A dog's bed
test: Where did Mum find the droppings and the chewed hole in the cereal packet? | In the bath | *In the cupboard | On the bus | In the garden
test: Which of these things would you keep in a kitchen cupboard? | *A packet of cereal | A bicycle | A sunny sky | A football
exam: Ollie put the console in the cupboard for a week. What does this tell you? | Ollie sold it at the shop | It was played with all day | It was left on the roof | *It was put away out of sight
